# sdkconfig overlay: virtual eFuses. DEVELOPMENT INSTRUMENT, NEVER A RELEASE BUILD.
#
# Applied on top of the base and board overlays with
#   tools\build.ps1 -Board <board> -Overlay firmware\sdkconfig.efuse-virtual.defaults
# and always into its OWN CARGO_TARGET_DIR (NOTYAS_TARGET_DIR), because the IDF
# build directory bakes in the merged sdkconfig and reusing the product board's
# directory would leave a stale, wrongly-configured bootloader behind it
# (firmware/README.md pitfalls 2 and 10).
#
# What it does: redirects every eFuse read and write to a RAM mirror.
# ESP-IDF's esp_efuse_utility_burn_chip_opt() has no real-burn arm at all under
# this option - it ORs the write registers into virt_blocks[] and logs
# "Virtual efuses enabled: Not really burning eFuses". The eFuse programming
# path is not merely skipped at run time, it is not compiled. This is what makes
# it safe to exercise the provisioning ladder on a board whose fuses must stay
# virgin (SECUREBOOT.md section 9, trap T3).
#
# What it does NOT do, and the reason a virtual run is never a substitute for a
# real one: the HMAC peripheral's key-purpose check is performed by hardware
# against the REAL eFuse block. A virtual block with purpose HMAC_UP produces a
# peripheral configuration error, not a MAC. The exercise proves the code path;
# only a burned block can prove the silicon path.
#
# CONFIG_EFUSE_VIRTUAL_KEEP_IN_FLASH is deliberately absent: it persists the
# mirror into an `emul_efuse` partition that the shipped partition table does
# not and must not contain (SECUREBOOT.md trap T4). Every virtual change dies
# with the power cycle, which is the property that makes this reversible.
CONFIG_EFUSE_VIRTUAL=y
